This test fails a few days per month and nobody knows why. Save /workspace/subscription.py:

from datetime import date

def next_billing_date(start):
    # bill on the same day next month; clamp to end of month when needed
    year = start.year + (start.month // 12)
    month = start.month % 12 + 1
    day = start.day
    while True:
        try:
            return date(year, month, day)
        except ValueError:
            day -= 1

def days_until_billing(start=None):
    start = start or date.today()
    return (next_billing_date(start) - start).days

and /workspace/tests/test_subscription.py:

from datetime import date, timedelta
from subscription import days_until_billing

def test_days_until_billing():
    assert 28 <= days_until_billing() <= 31

Explain in comments why this is flaky garbage: it depends on today's date; on Jan 31 the correct answer is 28 (billing Feb 28) and the assertion range is wrong for several real dates, plus it can never catch actual bugs. Demonstrate by monkeypatching or by calling days_until_billing(date(2026, 1, 31)) and (2026, 3, 31) and (2026, 2, 1) directly with exact expected values (compute them by hand: Jan 31 -> Feb 28 is 28; Mar 31 -> Apr 30 is 30; Feb 1 -> Mar 1 is 28 in 2026). Replace the flaky test with those deterministic cases, keep one today-based smoke test but make it assert only invariants that are ALWAYS true (result between 28 and 31 inclusive AND next_billing_date is after start), and add Dec -> Jan year rollover plus a leap-year Jan 31 2028 -> Feb 29 case. pytest green, then run it 20 times in a loop to show stability.
